WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.433
- Give it up for Nick Offerman!

00:00:06.053 --> 00:00:08.733
(ukulele playing)

00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:12.328
- ♪ Holidays on the Kelly Clarkson show ♪

00:00:12.328 --> 00:00:13.554
(crowd cheering)

00:00:13.554 --> 00:00:18.041
♪ Here in the San Fernando
well where the rain does snow ♪

00:00:18.041 --> 00:00:21.376
♪ I'd like to wrestle my
wife under the mistletoe ♪

00:00:21.376 --> 00:00:25.819
♪ But I'll leave it at that,
'cause this is a family show ♪

00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:26.819
- I love it!

00:00:29.384 --> 00:00:30.559
I love this!

00:00:30.559 --> 00:00:31.559
- Thank you.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.727
- Wait, did you- say hello!

00:00:35.727 --> 00:00:37.353
- Hello!

00:00:37.353 --> 00:00:38.670
- [Clarkson] Did you make that,

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:39.700
is that one of the things you've made?

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.344
- I did make this ukulele.

00:00:41.344 --> 00:00:42.360
- [Clarkson] What?

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.601
- It's garbage, actually.

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:44.690
(crowd laughs)

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:45.992
If you look up close.

00:00:45.992 --> 00:00:48.240
But it sounds like a ukulele.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.960
- [Clarkson] I was gonna
say it sounds great, yeah.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.924
- Did you see "The Beatles: Get Back"?

00:00:52.924 --> 00:00:54.374
- Oh yeah, yeah.

00:00:54.374 --> 00:00:57.015
- That was kind of based
on me and my song writing.

00:00:57.015 --> 00:00:58.610
(crowd and Clarkson laughing)

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.290
- The similarities are just uncanny.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.310
So wait, what else have you made recently?

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.190
- What I made is you look fantastic.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.430
- Oh thank you!

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.242
(crowd cheering)

00:01:08.242 --> 00:01:09.150
It's so nice to be here for the holidays.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.360
- We love Christmas.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.330
We were trying to go big for Christmas.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.423
I'm Texan, we don't go small ever.

00:01:14.423 --> 00:01:17.760
But I love that you make
things, I think it's so cool.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.270
And I don't know like a lot of people

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:20.750
that really do that in general anymore.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.710
I think that's so cool.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.800
- I think it's really healthy.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.380
I was brought up in a family
of people who make things

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.028
and Megan is amazing.

00:01:28.028 --> 00:01:29.550
Just a couple of days ago,

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.610
she made me the most amazing pecan pie.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:34.810
- [Clarkson] I love pecan pie.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:35.650
- I mean, I can't wait.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.010
There's still a couple of pieces left.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:37.953
So if we can keep things moving.

00:01:37.953 --> 00:01:40.934
- So that was rude, you didn't bring any.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:41.767
(crowd laughing)

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:42.600
- No I didn't.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:43.433
- Fine, fine.

00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:44.266
- And I wanna get home.

00:01:44.266 --> 00:01:46.180
- Well you said before that you and Megan,

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.623
y'all make each other gifts, right?

00:01:47.623 --> 00:01:48.456
- That's right.

00:01:48.456 --> 00:01:49.289
- So like what?

00:01:49.289 --> 00:01:50.400
Like what are some examples?

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.810
- Well, I love making her stuff.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.662
I mean, I make her furniture and you know,

00:01:54.662 --> 00:01:57.234
other things out of wood and sometimes,

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:59.280
we've been together 21 years-

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.559
- Wow, that's amazing.

00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:03.309
(crowd cheering)

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.760
- If I come up short, 'cause I like to do

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.430
traditional anniversary gifts and stuff,

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.180
but sometimes, you know that list

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.310
where it's like, your five is copper.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:14.870
- Or the wood is one.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.640
- Yeah, so one year was willow.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.210
So I made her a little
elf spoon out of willow,

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.700
out of the wood willow
and that went over big.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.410
And so then a few times, if
I don't have a good idea,

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.902
then I've made her a fork and a knife.

00:02:29.902 --> 00:02:30.735
(crowd laughing)

00:02:30.735 --> 00:02:31.810
- You lean back into the cutlery.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.386
- Yeah, and there's a
tea cup and a saucer.

00:02:34.386 --> 00:02:36.400
So I think eventually, you know,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.557
as long as I don't get
kicked out of the house,

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:41.758
she'll eventually have a full dinner set.

00:02:41.758 --> 00:02:44.331
- (laughing) I love her elves, I love it.

00:02:44.331 --> 00:02:46.210
Wait, so you made Amy Poehler,

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.333
is it correct that it
was a paddle out of doors

00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:51.290
and it was doors from the
"Parks and Rec" set, right?

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:52.123
- Yeah.

00:02:52.123 --> 00:02:52.956
- How did you do that?

00:02:52.956 --> 00:02:56.487
- When the show finished,
the entire bullpen

00:02:56.487 --> 00:02:59.102
of that great set for "Parks and Rec',

00:02:59.102 --> 00:03:01.476
all the trim was red oak that actually

00:03:01.476 --> 00:03:04.090
had come from Indiana to California.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.430
- I know, but they let you have it?

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:06.576
- Well, no, here's the thing.

00:03:06.576 --> 00:03:07.453
(crowd laughing)

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:08.430
- Oh.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.240
- And this is a real beef I have

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.878
with the corporate side of our business,

00:03:12.878 --> 00:03:14.820
is that because of liability,

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.360
you're not supposed to take
anything out of the dumpster.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:18.640
Like wood.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.030
- But it's wasteful.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.022
- It's super wasteful.

00:03:21.022 --> 00:03:24.428
So I've mentioned when they struck the set

00:03:24.428 --> 00:03:26.890
for "Parks and Rec', I
said to the guy driving

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.341
the truck to the landfill,

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:31.465
if you happen to hit a
speed bump near my shop

00:03:31.465 --> 00:03:34.565
and some of this red oak
falls onto the sidewalk,

00:03:34.565 --> 00:03:35.640
(crowd laughing)

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.980
I would love to turn it into canoe paddles

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.867
to give to the cast and the producers.

00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:40.700
- Which is so cool.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.024
- So, yeah, I mean-

00:03:42.024 --> 00:03:43.462
(crowd cheering)

00:03:43.462 --> 00:03:47.799
- I'm grateful that that
driver was somewhat reckless.

00:03:47.799 --> 00:03:49.150
(crowd laughing)

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.928
- Yeah, that's unfortunate.

00:03:50.928 --> 00:03:53.240
But what kind of music do you listen to

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.810
whenever you're making stuff?

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.330
- In the shop, the wood
shop is a great place

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.060
to play music because you know,

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.730
you're often sanding a table like this,

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:03.693
or painting varnish, you know,

00:04:03.693 --> 00:04:08.530
jobs that require some meditative moments.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.760
So the first music I listened to was

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.860
in my dad's basement shop.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.010
And I remember in the winter,

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:16.720
he'd have the wood stove going.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.740
And there was a radio station
out of Chicago called WJJD.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:24.990
And they would play like The
Mills Brothers and Tommy Dorsey

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.420
and Rosemary Clooney, Ella Fitzgerald,

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.548
sort of big band swing, jazz era.

00:04:32.548 --> 00:04:35.720
So that always makes me
feel really nostalgic.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:38.030
And like when I hear The Mills Brothers,

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:40.100
I get the smell of varnish.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:41.290
- Oh I love that nostalgia,

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.110
like it comes with memories, music.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.000
I love that.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.856
- But yeah, we listened
to all kinds of stuff.

00:04:45.856 --> 00:04:46.980
- I do that when I quilt.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.920
I put vinyl on whenever I
quilt, that's my big thing.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.140
I go to a classic
superstar like Carole King,

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.180
like that's a good one.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.930
- Totally, or early Neil Young.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.470
- Yeah, Neil Young, great.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.202
- Or any of those Laurel Canyon gang,

00:05:00.202 --> 00:05:02.130
Crosby, Stills, Nash.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.890
- It's like a total party happening

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:05.407
while you're making stuff.

00:05:05.407 --> 00:05:06.505
(crowd cheering)

00:05:06.505 --> 00:05:08.307
The new book is called, I love the title,

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:10.130
"Where the Deer and the Antelope Play",

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.880
and then there's more
words, I said it before,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.160
but what's it about,
like what inspired this?

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:14.993
I love this.

00:05:14.993 --> 00:05:17.530
- Well, it's about our
relationship with nature.

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:20.916
You know, I love to read
certain agrarian writers,

00:05:20.916 --> 00:05:24.405
which is a fancy word for good farmers.

00:05:24.405 --> 00:05:29.405
People who grow a crop or
livestock with an eye towards

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:32.763
how can we raise cattle
on this land in a way

00:05:32.763 --> 00:05:35.376
that we can keep doing
it that will sustain

00:05:35.376 --> 00:05:38.420
the nature on this land.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:41.220
And when I was reading,
I was like, oh yeah,

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.842
I'm really guilty, as most of us are

00:05:43.842 --> 00:05:46.752
with being lazily ignorant to like,

00:05:46.752 --> 00:05:49.721
who grows our food and
where does it come from?

00:05:49.721 --> 00:05:52.360
And are we taking care of the land?

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.210
- And what's being sprayed on it?

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:55.150
- Yeah, exactly.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.190
And so with a sense of humor,

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.187
I travel around, do a few different trips,

00:05:59.187 --> 00:06:01.522
and, you know, talk about our relationship

00:06:01.522 --> 00:06:03.520
with nature in this book.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:04.353
- [Clarkson] I love that.

00:06:04.353 --> 00:06:05.330
Well, and you grew up in nature,

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:06.700
you grew up on a farm, right?

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:10.557
- In a farm family, yeah, in
Illinois working for my family.

00:06:10.557 --> 00:06:12.850
- Did you have like all
those jobs, like my daughter,

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:16.340
she got excited, she came home
from our ranch in Montana,

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:17.369
and apparently one of her jobs,

00:06:17.369 --> 00:06:20.890
well her job was literally
mucking the stalls.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:25.760
So she just was shoveling
horse poop and she loved it.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.910
- Well, that's the thing,
that was my first job,

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:31.500
was shoveling out the pig waste.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.020
- Okay, this is the thing
we give children. (laughing)

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.030
I love it.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:35.863
- That's the thing.

00:06:35.863 --> 00:06:37.445
- Her arms were killing her, she was like-

00:06:37.445 --> 00:06:39.140
- It's work that needs doing,

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.820
but you can't really break anything,

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:42.600
like you can't screw it up.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.240
- Yeah, I think it's really good for kids

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.390
to grow up doing stuff like that.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.254
But did you ever see, you know,

00:06:47.254 --> 00:06:50.180
I know you took a road
trip, you and Megan,

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:51.400
so did y'all ever see any like

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.660
deer or antelope on the range?

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:54.730
- We did actually, yeah.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:57.800
I mean, in parts of Texas and Arizona,

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.220
while I was out hiking,

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:02.323
we would run into both deer and antelope.

00:07:02.323 --> 00:07:05.890
They didn't seem super playful,
they were more businesslike.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:06.820
- Yeah, like get away.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.700
- They were looking for food.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.870
- So whose idea it was
for y'all to travel,

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:12.320
because you traveled
around in an Airstream,

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.000
which I love an Airstream by the way.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.730
- Oh man, they're so groovy.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:18.760
Megan is the curator of
all things in our house.

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.962
Like if work needs doing
that requires tools,

00:07:21.962 --> 00:07:24.326
then it's in my wheel house.

00:07:24.326 --> 00:07:29.326
Otherwise, if it's picking
anything with taste or culture,

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.520
we're like, honey, please tell me

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.040
what movie we're gonna see.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.832
So it was her idea to go
see our families in Oklahoma

00:07:38.832 --> 00:07:41.292
and Illinois for Thanksgiving last year.

00:07:41.292 --> 00:07:43.590
So we went, we thought
we were gonna go buy

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:46.460
one of those Sprinter vans
that's like a little Airstream.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.179
And we ended up leaving
with the biggest Airstream

00:07:49.179 --> 00:07:50.860
they had for sale.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.100
- Oh my God, we're the same.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.530
And then did you get out
there and have any issues?

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.420
'Cause that's the thing, that
you think this is so fun.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.702
And then you get out there,
and you're like, oh no.

00:07:59.702 --> 00:08:00.535
(crowd laughing)

00:08:00.535 --> 00:08:02.250
Something goes wrong,
did anything go wrong?

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:03.308
- Not completely wrong.

00:08:03.308 --> 00:08:06.687
But like, I was busy
actually shooting a season

00:08:06.687 --> 00:08:08.608
of "Making It" with Amy Poehler

00:08:08.608 --> 00:08:11.452
while we were about to leave on this trip.

00:08:11.452 --> 00:08:13.800
So I didn't have time to sort of-

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.390
- All I'm hearing are
excuses right now. (laughing)

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.523
- Okay, here comes some more.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:22.268
So I was like, I've handled
equipment my whole life,

00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:24.655
so I'm sure I'll figure it out as we go.

00:08:24.655 --> 00:08:27.888
But the very first night we
got to the place too late,

00:08:27.888 --> 00:08:29.526
so they wouldn't let us in.

00:08:29.526 --> 00:08:31.330
They made us stay in a parking lot.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.303
And I wasn't familiar, you've
got an electrical system,

00:08:34.303 --> 00:08:36.998
you got plumbing, you got septic tanks,

00:08:36.998 --> 00:08:40.100
you got propane, all
kinds of stuff going on.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:43.250
And I hadn't had the time
to figure it all out yet.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:47.410
So the first night we spent
freezing our butts off.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:49.030
Like we couldn't turn the heat on

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:50.350
because we didn't have electricity.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:51.745
There's nothing to plug into.

00:08:51.745 --> 00:08:53.581
So very quickly I learned how

00:08:53.581 --> 00:08:56.220
to get on top of those systems,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.723
and fortunately, we're still married.

00:08:58.723 --> 00:08:59.662
(crowd laughing)

00:08:59.662 --> 00:09:00.620
- I was going to say, did you learn that

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:01.510
because of your desire

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:05.250
or her annoyance at you
not knowing? (laughing)

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:06.738
- The two are not unconnected.

00:09:06.738 --> 00:09:07.850
(crowd laughing)

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:09.500
- I love that, I love that moment.

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.163
I can just see both of
you, like this is so great.

00:09:11.163 --> 00:09:12.840
This is a time together.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:13.880
You're like freezing in it.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.670
So wait in the book you
revealed that you and your wife

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:18.310
both have a connection to Rob Lowe.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:19.500
And I know that connection,

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.880
but everyone else doesn't, so explain.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:24.670
- Well, you know, when you get together

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:26.480
with another actor and this is true,

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:29.359
and this is my whole life,
when you start dating someone,

00:09:29.359 --> 00:09:32.814
one of the things you do, you
have an awareness of like,

00:09:32.814 --> 00:09:36.074
oh, by the way, I was in
a play with this person

00:09:36.074 --> 00:09:40.793
or you like, you figure out
who you've both kissed or like-

00:09:42.212 --> 00:09:43.530
- This is a conversation?

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:46.798
- Yeah, it's a thing so
that, you know, I mean,

00:09:46.798 --> 00:09:50.225
it's true of any new
relationship where it's-

00:09:50.225 --> 00:09:52.229
- Not me, I don't wanna know any past.

00:09:52.229 --> 00:09:53.062
(crowd laughing)

00:09:53.062 --> 00:09:53.930
- But eventually you learn about

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:55.680
each other's significant others.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.880
And in this case, because
he's a marquee name,

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.130
it came up at some point that Megan,

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:03.000
in the movie "About Last Night",

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.000
had had this crazy throat
kissing scene with Rob Lowe,

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:10.195
where she plays this floozy,

00:10:10.195 --> 00:10:14.476
like trying to distract
him from Demi Moore.

00:10:14.476 --> 00:10:17.620
And they have this crazy
French kissing scene.

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:19.594
And at some point I randomly saw it.

00:10:19.594 --> 00:10:22.594
We were at a hotel and
it happened to be on TV.

00:10:22.594 --> 00:10:24.927
And it was so traumatic where I was like-

00:10:24.927 --> 00:10:26.460
(crowd laughing)

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.050
- What are you doing!

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:31.360
- I mean, it was 15 years
later, but I said, honey,

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.302
you clearly are in love with Rob Lowe.

00:10:33.302 --> 00:10:34.500
(Clarkson and crowd laughing)

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.900
This is gonna be a situation between us.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.300
And so it always kind of stuck with me.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.117
But then until some years
later on "Parks and Recreation"

00:10:43.117 --> 00:10:47.920
in an episode where I think
Rob Lowe's character finds out

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.521
that it's Ron Swanson's birthday

00:10:49.521 --> 00:10:53.195
and he ends up kissing me on
the mouth in this episode.

00:10:53.195 --> 00:10:54.390
- Both been there.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:57.248
- At which point I was
like, okay, honey, I get it.

00:10:57.248 --> 00:10:58.650
(Clarkson and crowd laughing)

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:00.272
- It's even.

00:11:00.272 --> 00:11:02.240
- Everything's cool, everything's fine.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:06.044
And now, if you step back
a little ways and squint,

00:11:06.044 --> 00:11:08.569
you could say that we've had a threesome.

00:11:08.569 --> 00:11:11.020
(Clarkson laughing)

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:11.930
That's how it is in Hollywood.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:13.782
- That's what your book
should have been called.

00:11:13.782 --> 00:11:14.615
(laughing)

00:11:14.615 --> 00:11:18.085
Our threesome with Rob Lowe. (laughing)

00:11:18.085 --> 00:11:20.280
That's amazing, I love that.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:22.700
I don't know if I'd
wanna know all of that.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.952
Not that it bothers me, I just mean like

00:11:24.952 --> 00:11:27.120
you can't help your past,
so why talk about it?

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.902
- It is weird, and especially
if your wife is a gorgeous,

00:11:30.902 --> 00:11:35.491
you know, star they keep coming
out where you'll be watching

00:11:35.491 --> 00:11:38.359
a show and she'll be like, oh, actually,

00:11:38.359 --> 00:11:43.359
I was engaged to this guy,
you know, and you're like.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:44.433
- Oh my God.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:47.553
And you're like, no, it's fine.

00:11:47.553 --> 00:11:49.493
- It's fine, honey.

00:11:49.493 --> 00:11:50.326
- (laughing) That's amazing.

00:11:50.326 --> 00:11:51.847
Well, Nick's book is called

00:11:51.847 --> 00:11:53.550
"Where the Deer and the Antelope Play:

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:56.110
the Pastoral Observations
of One Ignorant American

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:57.590
Who Loves to Walk Outside."

00:11:57.590 --> 00:11:59.980
It's out now, I cannot wait to read it.

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:01.030
I love you so much.

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:02.661
You're literally one of my
favorite people on the show.

00:12:02.661 --> 00:12:03.761
Oh, thank you so much.

